Design a Spark SQL/Hive query to extract the following information from a given dataset:

1. Total number of orders placed by each customer.
2. Average order value per customer.
3. Top 5 products with the highest unit prices.
4. Number of orders placed on each day of the week (Monday, Tuesday, etc.), grouped by month.

Assume that the dataset contains the following columns:

- Order ID (order_id)
- Customer ID (customer_id)
- Order date (order_date)
- Product ID (product_id)
- Units purchased (units)
- Unit price (unit_price)

The dataset is stored in a Hive table named "orders_data". Ensure that your query utilizes Spark SQL/Hive and utilizes efficient optimizations to handle large datasets.﻿#python

import pyspark.sql as sql

# Reading the data from Hive table
spark = sql.SparkSession.builder.getOrCreate()
orders_df = spark.read.table("orders_data")

# 1) Total number of orders placed by each customer
customer_order_count = orders_df.groupby("customer_id") \
    .count() \
    .withColumnRenamed("count", "total_orders")

# 2) Average order value per customer
avg_order_value = orders_df.select("customer_id", "unit_price", "units") \
    .groupby("customer_id") \
    .agg(sql.functions.sum("unit_price" * "units").alias("total_revenue"),
         sql.functions.count("order_id").alias("total_orders")) \
    .select("customer_id",
            sql.functions.col("total_revenue") / sql.functions.col("total_orders").cast(sql.types.DoubleType).alias("avg_order_value"))

# 3) Top 5 products with the highest unit prices
top_products = orders_df.select("product_id", "unit_price") \
    .groupby("product_id") \
    .agg(sql.functions.sum("unit_price").alias("total_price")) \
    .select("product_id", "total_price") \
    .orderBy("total_price", ascending=False) \
    .limit(5)

# 4) Number of orders placed on each day of the week (Monday, Tuesday, etc.), grouped by month
from pyspark.sql.functions import dayofweek, month, date_format, lit

orders_by_weekday_and_month = orders_df.withColumn("week_day", dayofweek("order_date")) \
    .withColumn("month", month("order_date")) \
    .withColumn("formatted_date", date_format("order_date", " yyyy-MM- dd")) \
    .groupby("month", "formatted_date", "week_day") \
    .count() \
    .drop("formatted_date")

orders_by_weekday_and_month = orders_by_weekday_and_month.withColumnRenamed("count", "num_orders") \
    .select("month", "week_day", "num_orders") \
    .orderBy("month", "week_day")

# Combining the results
result_df = customer_order_count.join(avg_order_value, "customer_id", "inner") \
    .drop(avg_order_value.customer_id) \
    .join(top_products, top_products.product_id == avg_order_value.product_id, "inner") \
    .drop(avg_order_value.total_orders) \
    .join(orders_by_weekday_and_month, [
        customer_order_count.customer_id == orders_by_weekday_and_month.month,
        lit(1).cast("int").alias("val1") > 0], "left") \
    .drop(orders_by_weekday_and_month.month)

print("Results:")
result_df.show()

