Votre serveur Express est maintenant configuré avec TypeScript. Vous pouvez commencer à écrire votre propre logique côté serveur en utilisant les bonnes pratiques de TypeScript, y compris le typage des fonctions, des fonctions d'API, etc. Tous les fichiers TypeScript seront compilés en JavaScript standard dans le dossier `dist` lorsque vous exécuterez `npm run build`. Cette étape est importante avant de lancer votre serveur avec la commande `npm run start`.user﻿# Can you tell me more about how to handle middleware with TypeScript in Express? Maybe with an example?﻿# Sure! Handling middleware in Express with TypeScript is quite straightforward. Here's an example of how to implement middleware in a TypeScript Express server:

1. First, let's set up our Express server and a basic middleware function that logs its name:

   ```typescript
   import express, { Request, Response } from 'express';

   const app = express();

   function logMiddleware(req: Request, res: Response, next: express.NextFunction) {
     console.log(`[${new Date().toISOString()}] ${req.method} ${req.originalUrl}`);
     next();
   }

   app.use(logMiddleware);

   app.get('/', (req: Request, res: Response) => {
     res.send('Hello world!');
   });

   const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;
   app.listen(port, () => {
     console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${port}`);
   });
   ```

   We define a new function called `logMiddleware` that logs the date and request method/URL, and then calls the `next()` function to pass control to the next middleware or the request handler (the callback passed to `app.get()` in this case). We then call `app.use()` to apply this middleware to all incoming requests. 2. Let's say you want to add another middleware that only logs the request method and URL if the request URL starts with `/api`. You can create a new middleware function:

   ```typescript
   import express, { Request, Response } from 'express';

   const app = express();

   function logApiMiddleware(req: Request, res: Response, next: express.NextFunction) {
     if (req.originalUrl.startsWith('/api')) {
       console.log(`[${new Date().toISOString()}] ${req.method} ${req.originalUrl}`);
     }
     next();
   }

   app.use(logMiddleware);
   app.use(logApiMiddleware);

   app.get('/', (req: Request, res: Response) => {
     res.send('Hello world!');
   });

   app.get('/api/users', (req: Request, res: Response) => {
     res.send('API: List of users');
   });

   const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;
   app.listen(port, () => {
     console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${port}`);
   });
   ```

   We have added a new middleware function called `logApiMiddleware` that logs information only if the request URL starts with `/api`. Then we call `app.use()` again to register this middleware, ensuring that both middleware run for every incoming request. 3. You can also create a generic middleware that can be applied to various routes. For example, let's create a middleware that adds a custom header to the response:

   ```typescript
   import express, { Request, Response } from 'express';

   const app = express();

   function addHeaderMiddleware(req: Request, res: Response, next: express.NextFunction) {
     res.setHeader('X-My-Header', 'Hello from middleware!');
     next();
   }

   app.use(logMiddleware);
   app.use(logApiMiddleware);
   app.use(addHeaderMiddleware); // apply this middleware to all routes

   app.get('/', (req: Request, res: Response) => {
     res.send('Hello world!');
   });

   app.get('/api/users', (req: Request, Response) => {
     res.send('API: List of users');
     res.setHeader('X-My-Other-Header', 'Hi again!'); // overriding the middleware header
   });

   const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;
   app.listen(port, () => {
     console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${port}`);
   });
   ```

   This middleware adds a header to all responses. To apply this to all routes, we simply call `app.use()` with the middleware function. However, we can override this header on a per-route basis, as illustrated by the users route.

   That's it! You can use the same middleware concepts and apply them in many different ways, depending on your specific use case.

